          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "You should discuss your diagnosis with your healthcare provider and follow their guidance for care.\n\nA diagnosis of type 2 diabetes indicates that your body struggles to regulate blood glucose levels due to insulin resistance and possibly insufficient insulin production by the pancreas. This results in elevated blood glucose levels and can develop gradually over time (source: \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management,\" \"General clinical reference\"). The condition is often associated with factors like obesity, physical inactivity, and genetics.\n\nFor first-line therapy, current guidelines suggest that the initial treatment typically involves metformin, which is a medication. This should be paired with lifestyle modifications, such as dietary changes and increased physical activity. The selection of medication may also depend on other individual health conditions (source: \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management,\" \"National diabetes association standards of care\").\n\nIn terms of coding, type 2 diabetes is classified under the ICD-10 code E11. This code is used for diabetes classification regardless of whether insulin is needed (source: \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories\"). For further details about coding and management, consider consulting local health resources or coding manuals.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
